\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
There are several R packages which use the hazard function as their underlying
tool for the analysis of multistate time to event models, a class that we will
broadly refer to as multi-state hazard models (MSH). These include for instance
the dtms, icmstate, msm, mstate, nhm and survival packages, among others.
This vignette is focused on the mshmm package, which is designed to deal with
intermittently observed multistate data.  
The primary cmsh function accomodates a
range of range of outcomes from simple interval censored (panel) data to a full
hidden Markov model, and hence the portamanteau name of (multi-state hazard +
hidden Markov model) or MSHMM for the package.
The user interface for mshmm is heavily influence by that for the multistate
models supported by the survival package, and we often use these models as
a contrast.

\subsection{Aside: counting process and timeline data}
The functions in the survival package that deal with multistate data
(coxph, survcheck, survfit, survSplit) allow for two styles of data.
For a data set in \emph{counting process} form
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each line has a subject id along with a time interval (t1, t2] to which
    this observation applies, a transition (outcome) variable, and covariates.
  \item Covariates apply over the entire interval, from start to finish,
    likewise for stratification variables, if present.
  \item Transitions to another state occur at the end of the interval.
\end{itemize}
For the newer \emph{timeline} form
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each line of the data has a subject id and time value pair.
  \item Other variables on the line are information that was measured at that
    time point.
  \item Values that carry forward from line to line, i.e., fixed covariates
    such as sex, may also be repeated.
\end{itemize}

A timeline data set is actually more natural in the sense that it mimics the 
way in which data is gathered. 
It also may be easier to create, since one can use a wider variety of R tools.
For ordinary survival, however, a Kaplan-Meier say,
data in timeline form requires two rows of data per subject:
the first observation at time 0 will have baseline covariates along with a
status of alive; a second
observation at the time of death or last follow-up will have the status at that
time point (alive/dead).
This is different than the traditional way that survival data is coded,
i.e., a single observation with a time/status variable pair.
The counting process format for the survival package evolved out of
simple survival
and so retains that instance as a special case, i.e., one line per subject
for the simple cases of alive/dead or competing risks.

The data sets in the \code{mshmm} package (and the msm package as well) are
all in timeline format, and this is the form required by the cmsh and msm
functions.
For survival functions we need to use
\code{Surv2} rather than \code{Surv} as a marker to inform the routine that 
the input data is in timeline form, for cmsh this is not necessary.

<<dem1>>=
subset(dementia, ptnum %in% 3:4, c(ptnum, age, male, educ, cmc, centiloid, dx))
@ 
 
A further observation is that a timeline dataset will have many missing 
values, e.g., blood pressure would not normally be known on the day of death,
especially if death date is obtained from other sources.
One consequence is that the usual first step of other modeling functions such
as \code{lm}, i.e., to remove any data row that contains missing values as the
very first step, is not taken by the cmsh function.  The handling of missings
is more nuanced.

\subsection{Dementia data}
To introduce the methods we will use a simple example based on the 
\code{dementia} data set found in the package, this data will be
used a running example throughout the vignette.
This data set is a randomized data set (all variables perturbed by noise),
itself based on a random subset of the data used in the analyses found 
in \cite{Jack22}, and based on the Mayo Clinic Study of Aging (MCSA).
A description of the MCSA can be found in Roberts et al \cite{Roberts08}.
In brief, the MCSA is an age and sex stratified sample of subjects from 
Olmsted County, Minnesota.
After recruitment, subjects are invited to return for a study
visit every 15 months, with the goal of understanding the evolution of
cognitive decline, along with various markers of pathologies that may underlie
said decline. 
As patients leave the study there have been new waves of recruitment, with the
goal of maintaining an active set of approximatedly 2--3 thousand subjects.
Although the dementia data set is closely modeled on an MCSA sample,
always remember that this is not real data. It is hoped that it will be useful
for both illustration and research on multistate methods, but is not a 
proper tool for clinical decision making.

<<first>>=
library(survival)
library(mshmm)
library(splines2, warn.conflicts= FALSE)
options(mc.cores= floor(parallel::detectCores()*.8)) #don't use them all
options(show.significant.stars = FALSE)  # display statistical intellegence
table2 <- function(...) table(..., useNA= "ifany")  # show missings in tables
@

The code above starts with declarations.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item The mshmm library depends on survival
  \item The splines2 library includes an updated copy of the nsk function, first
    piloted with survival and now deprecated there (the splines2 version is
    superior); the warn.conflicts option stops a warning that the survival
    version has been preempted.
  \item The mshmm code makes use of parallel processing to speed up 
    computation, the mc.cores option attempts to set a reasonable default.
  \item The table2 function is simply a version of table that will show missings
    by default
\end{itemize}

<<dementia1>>=
dxcount <- table2(dementia$dx)
dxcount
check1 <- survcheck(Surv2(age, dx)~1, data=dementia, id=ptnum)
check1$transitions
@ 

The \code{dx} variable in the data set is a portamanteau of results.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item For an on study visit the patient is assessed as cognitively
    unimpaired (CU), mild congitive impairment (MCI) or dementia.
  \item Patients who have not returned in over 30 months and are not yet 
    known to be demented are placed on a queue for review of
    their patient history by a set of trained nurse abstracters. 
    The reviewers are able to assess
    demented vs. non-demented (ND) status from physician notes and diagnoses at
    standard office visits, but since such visits do not include formal 
    cognitive testing it is not possible to distinguish CU from MCI; 
    the nurse abstracter reviews are coded as 
    non-demented (ND) or off-study dementia (off-dem) in the \code{dx} 
    variable. 
    Unfortunately, if there are multiple such reviews only the last 
    was retained in the study data base;
    this leads to zeros in the 2 by 2 nondem/off-dem portion of the transitions
    table above.
  \item About 45\% of the subjects have a final row of information containing 
    only a status of alive/dead.  This might be the result of an 
    outpatient visit such as a vaccination, from patient correspondence, 
    or electronic sources.
    A very small number of in study visits are missing the
    diagnosis (17), these also have a diagnosis of alive.
\end{enumerate}
Each row of data corresponds to exactly one of these three types of contact.
The MCSA is a population sample, so it is not surprising that most of
the study visits 
(\Sexpr{paste0(dxcount[2], '/', sum(dxcount[2:4]), ' = ', round(100*dxcount[2]/sum(dxcount[2:4])))} %\), 
have a status of cognitively unimpaired.

\begin{figure}
<<figjack1, echo=FALSE>>=
astate <- c("not\ndemented", "dementia", "death")
amat <- matrix(0, 3, 3, dimnames=list(astate, astate))
amat[1,2] <- amat[1,3] <- amat[2,3] <- 1
statefig(rbind(2,1), amat)
@ 
  \caption{State space for the simple dementia model.}
  \label{figjack1}
\end{figure}

The 2022 Jack study used a simpler three state categorization shown in figure
\ref{figjack1}, of non-demented (ND), ever dementia, and death.  
Create a dx2 variable containing this simpler coding.

<<mcsadx2>>=
temp1 <- c(1,2,2,3,2,3,4)[as.numeric(dementia$dx)]
temp2 <- factor(temp1, 1:4, c("alive", "ND", "dementia", "death"))
table2(temp2, dx=dementia$dx) # check
# Create "ever demented"
# (There must be a less mysterious way to do this than unlist(tapply(...)
temp3 <- unlist(tapply(temp1, dementia$ptnum, 
                       function(x) ifelse(x==1, 1, cummax(x))))
dementia$dx2 <- factor(temp3, 1:4, c("alive", "ND", "dementia", "death"))

survcheck(Surv2(age, dx2) ~ 1, data=dementia, id= ptnum)$transitions
@

We see that the recode has been successful: the 33 dementia:MCI transtions
have been removed.
(Eleven subjects have ND to dementia to ND, so the total number of transitions
to dementia also decreases).

As a first look use simple Poisson regression to estimate the death and
dementia rates over time.
For fitting a glm model it is easier to first cast the data into
counting process form,
where each observation represents a time interval.
The \code{fromtimeline} function replaces age with age1 and age2, and adds
the istate variable.  The resultant data set will have one less line
per subject.
By default the counting process data set does not treat repeated visits in the
same state as an event. 
The fromtimeline function also applies last value carried forward (LVCF)
logic to replace missing values.

<<mcsadx3>>=
# make counting process data
demc <- fromtimeline(Surv2(age, dx2) ~ ., dementia, id=ptnum)
subset(dementia, ptnum==14, c(ptnum, age, years, male, educ, cmc, dx2))
subset(demc, ptnum==14, c(ptnum, age1, age2, years, male, educ, 
                         cmc, istate, dx2))
@

A well known trick for fitting poisson event rates to count data is to use an
\code{offset} statement.  We observe events, but are interested in modeling
event rates. Let $y$ be the Poisson count and $r$ the rate. We want to restrict
the rate to be positive, and thus
\begin{align*}
E(y) &= E(rt) \\
     &= e^{X\beta}t
     &= e^{X\beta + \log(t)}
\end{align*}
The observation time for each subject appears a covariate $\log(t)$ with a
known coefficient of 1, a covariate with known coefficient is known as an
offset.

<<mcsadx4>>=
# the width of the interval
demc$width <- demc$age2- demc$age1
pfit0 <- glm(I(dx2=="death") ~ age1 + male + offset(log(width)),
             family="poisson", data=demc)
pfit1 <- glm(I(dx2=="death") ~ ns(age1, df=4) + male + offset(log(width)),
             family="poisson", data=demc)
pfit2 <- glm(I(dx2=="dementia") ~ ns(age1, df=4) + male + offset(log(width)),
             family="poisson", data=demc, 
             subset= (demc$istate=="ND" & demc$dx2 != "death"))
# in a final ND:death interval a subject is not at risk for *detected*
#  dementia, so are left out of the denominator

dummy <- expand.grid(age1=55:99, male=0:1, width=1000)
yhat1 <- matrix(predict(pfit1, newdata=dummy, type='response'), ncol=2)
yhat2 <- matrix(predict(pfit2, newdata=dummy, type='response'), ncol=2)
matplot(55:99, cbind(yhat0, yhat1, yhat2), type='l', lwd=2, lty=c(1,1,1,1,2,2), 
        col=1:2, log='y', ylim=c(.1, 1000), lwd=c(1,1,2,2,2,2),
        xlab="Age", ylab="Rate per 1000")
legend(55, 1000, c("Death, male", "Death, female", "Dementia, male", 
                   "Dementia, female"), col=2:1, lty=c(1,1,2,2), lwd=2,
       bty='n')

# Ever death, ever dementia
aj1 <- survfit(Surv(age1, age2, dx2) ~male, id=ptnum, istate=istate,
               p0= c(1,0,0), start.time=60, 
               data=demc, subset= (istate=="ND"))
plot(aj1[,-1], col=1:2, lty=c(1,1,2,2), lwd=2, xmax=100,
     xlab="Age", ylab="State")
legend("topleft", c("death w/o dem, male", "death w/o dem, female", 
                    "ever dementia, male", "ever dementia, female"),
       col=2:1, lty=c(2,2,1,1), lwd=2, bty='n')
@

Above age 70 the hazard for death is essentially log-linear, but the
hazard of dementia is far from log-linear. 
Death rates for males are \code{exp(coef(pfit1)['male'])} about 1.37
times the female rate, while there is little apparent difference
in the sex specific dementia rate (1.05, p=.5).

For the 2022 analysis the effect of amyloid level on dementia rates was a
question of primary interest; amyloid levels were categorized as 
low/moderate/high/missing.  The presence of one or more $\ee$ alleles
in the APOE gene, along with male sex, CMC score and possibly education 
were also known to have an effet on dementia and/or death rates.  
Amyloid level is measured via positron emission tomography (PET) using
carbon-11 labeled Pittsburgh compound B (PiB) as the tracer, with resulting
levels recorded in centiloid units.
Amyloid is time-dependent, use last value carried forward (LVCF) to replace
any missing values before creating the missing category.

<<ifit>>=
ddata <- dementia  # work on a local copy
temp1 <- with(ddata, lvcf(ptnum, centiloid))
temp2 <- ifelse(is.na(temp1), 0,
                as.numeric(cut(temp1, c(-100, 22, 68, 400))))
ddata$agrp <- factor(temp2, c(1:3, 0), c("low", "moderate", "high", "missing"))
ddata$edu4 <- pmax(8, dementia$educ)/4

# Fit a transition based MSH model
dfit0 <- coxph(list(Surv2(age, dx2) ~ male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4 + cmc,
                    1:2 ~ male*(any.e4 + agrp)),
               data= ddata, id= ptnum)

# Now fit an 'observed state' MSH model
states <- c("ND", "dementia", "death")
qmat <- matrix(0, 3, 3, dimnames=list(from=states, to=states))
qmat[1,2] <- qmat[1,3] <- qmat[2,3] <- 1
qmat

options(mc.cores = floor(.8* parallel::detectCores()))

# initial coefficients based on a fit of the Minnesota rates
# (all the time intervals are 1 year, so no offset is needed)
tdata <- data.frame(age= 50:95,
                    rate= 365.25 *survexp.mn[51:96, 1, "2010"])
gfit <- glm(rate ~ age, quasipoisson, data=tdata)
icoef <- cbind(coef(gfit), coef(gfit), coef(gfit))

dfit1a <- cmsh(Surv(age, dx2) ~ age, data=ddata, id=ptnum, 
               qmatrix=qmat, init = icoef)
dfit1 <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, dx2) ~ age+ male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4 + cmc,
                    1:2 ~ male*(any.e4 + agrp)),
               data= ddata, id= ptnum, qmatrix= qmat, init=dfit1a)

c(coxph = diff(dfit0$loglik), cmsh= diff(dfit1$loglik))
@

Calling sequences for the two methods are similar (on purpose). There are
5 variables that affect the event rate, with two further interaction
terms for the ND:dementia transition. The primary changes at this point
are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The state based likelihood requires a design matrix $Q$. 
    This is a square
    matrix that contains zeros for any transitions that can not occur, and 1
    for those that can.  (Actually, any positive value in (0,2) is allowed: the
    statefig routine uses the same matrix, where values that differ from 1 lead
    to curved lines.)
  \item The baseline hazard is explicit rather than implicit. In the above
    fit we assume that the underlying hazard function 
    $\lambda_k(a)= \exp(\beta_{0k} +\beta_{1k}a)$ for each of the 3 hazard
    functions $k= 1,2,3$, $a$ = age. The cumulative hazard plot shown earlier
    would suggest that this linearity assumption is questionable for the 
    ND:dementia transtion.
  \item Initial values are critical. The default of 0 for all coefficients
    corresponds in this case to a probability of exp(-30) for a 50 year old
    to reach age 80 in a non-demented state, and thus a terrible starting guess.
    (No transitions for 30 years but an assumed mean time to transition of
    exp(0)=1).
    We have found it useful to first fit a model with only the intercept
    and age, then use those results as starting values for further models.
    (Replace age with the appropriate time scale for the given data, of course.)
  \item Compuations are much slower. If multiple CPU cores are available,
    use them.
\end{itemize}

The difference in log likelhood between a baseline and final model is of
similar magnitude for the two fits. Figure \ref{hr2022}, later in the document,
will show that the coefficients are similar as well.
Both fits include time dependent covariates (amyloid and CMC), which precludes
drawing probabiliy in state curves, however, which are
an essential component of a multistate analysis.
This and other important extensions will be topics in further sections, the
above is intended to give an initial view.


\section{Examples, part 1}
\subsection{The colon cancer trial}
The \code{colon} data set in the survival package contains the results of
a trial of three treatments for stage B/C colon cancer.
This is a simple 3 state model of entry, progression, and death.
The data set is organized with 2 rows per subject, the first containing the
progression outcome and the second the death outcome.
As a first step, however, we need to decide what to do with the 5 subjects
for which progression and death were recorded on the same day.
There is nothing wrong with this, per se: the author has for instance
had studies where progression was discovered at autopsy, and one might
reasonably expect an interval censored model to accomodate it.
However, it has been our experience that ``observation'' of two states on the 
same day is more often a data creation error, and the \code{cmsh} routine
currently flags it as such.
To resolve the conflict we will shift those progressions one day earlier.
(Or even .001 day if one wants.)
Also beware the two for whom their progression date is also their last 
follow-up time, so as not to add a superfluous ``censoring'' row at the end.

<<colon1>>=
temp1 <- subset(colon, etype==1) # progression
temp2 <- subset(colon, etype==2) # death
ties <- (temp1$time==temp2$time & temp1$status==1 & temp2$status==1)
temp1$time[ties] <- temp1$time[ties] -1 

# Make the timeline data set, we will use only a few of the predictors
#  The baseline data can use the row for either etype=1 or 2, as the covariates 
#  are identical
baseline <- subset(temp1, , c(id, rx, sex, age, node4, extent))
baseline$time   <- 0
baseline$status <- -1
prog <- subset(temp1, status==1, c(id, time, status)) # progression
dfu  <- subset(temp2, temp1$status==0 | time > temp1$time, 
               c(id, time, status)) # death or last fu
dfu$status <- dfu$status *2  # recode as 0/2
temp3 <- merge(baseline, prog, all=TRUE)
cdata <- merge(temp3, dfu, all=TRUE)
cdata$status <- factor(cdata$status, c(0, -1, 1, 2), 
                       c("censor", "stable", "progression", "death"))
subset(cdata, id<4)
@

Subject 1 has both progression and death, subject 2
is censored without progression at 3087 days.

One of the necessary conditions for a valid model, per underlying counting
process theory, is the concept of a predictable process.
In essense, an outcome observed at time $t$ cannot depend on any predictors
measured at or after time $t$. 
The cmsh function enforces this: any observed outcome on a given line of data is
predicted using information from prior rows of data.
One consequence is that prediction covariates found on the final data row for 
a subject have no impact. 
A second action within cmsh is to use the most recently observed value for
any predictor; it is not necessary to fill in the values for treatment, age at
entry, etc in every row of the data set.
It is a bit of a semantic argument whether each row of a timeline data set 
should contain only values \emph{measured} at that time or all
values \emph{known} at that time, i.e., whether fixed covariates like sex
should be propogated across all rows. 
The cmsh function is happy with either, and more capable data set creation tools
than the basic R merge used above may well fill these in. 

As a first look compute the transition matrix.
Then compute the probability in state and cumulative
hazard curves. Many of the survival routines can accept timeline data via
the \code{Surv2} operator.

<<coloncount>>=
survcheck(Surv2(time, status) ~1, cdata, id=id)

aj1 <- survfit(Surv2(time, status) ~ 1, cdata, id=id)
plot(aj1, col=1:3, xscale=365, lwd=2,
     xlab="Years post randomization", ylab="Current state")
legend(400, .54, c("Entry", "Progression", "Death"), lty=1, lwd=2, col=1:3,
       bty='n')
plot(aj1, cumhaz=TRUE, col=1:3, xscale=365, lwd=2,  
     xlab="Years post randomization", ylab= "Cumulative Hazard")
legend(1500, 2, c("Entry:Progression", "Entry:Death", "Progression:Death"),
       col=1:3, lwd=2, lty=1, bty='n')
@ 

In this study about 46\% (423/929) of the subjects had no transitions,
i.e., were still in the stable state at last observation, 
both death and progression was observed for 45\% (414/929).  
Only a small number died without
progression (38) or were in the progression state at last follow-up.

The cumulative hazards at 8 years are about .7, .1 and 4 for the transitions
of entry to progression, entry to death and progression to death,
which provide reasonable starting estimates for the baseline hazards of the
interval censored fit. 
Using years as the time scale will give more interpretable coefficients, so we
add that variable.

Let us start with a simple Cox model fit to serve as comparison.
<<colonb>>=
cfit0 <- coxph(Surv2(time, status) ~ rx, data=cdata, id=id)
cfit0

dummy <- data.frame(rx=c("Obs", "Lev+5FU"))
surv0 <- survfit(cfit0, newdata= dummy, p0=c(1,0,0))
dim(surv0)
plot(surv0, lty=1:2, col=c(1,1,3,3,2,2), lwd=2, xscale=365.25,
     xlab="Years post chemo", ylab="Probabilty in state")
legend("topright", c("Stable, placebo", "Stable, 5FU+Lev", 
                     "Progression, placebo", "Progresion, 5FU+Lev",
                     "Death, placebo", "Death, 5fu+Lev"), 
       lty=1:2, lwd=2, col=c(1,1,3,3,2,2),, bty='n')
@

The hazard ratios show that combined treatment is more effective than either
Levamisole alone or placebo with respect to progression, 
and that the latter two are very similar.  The predicted probability in state
curves bear this out, and have a shape similar to the Aalen-Johansen.
Since probability in state curves have to add to 1, we often leave out the
``still in initial state'' ones.
Now recreate the fit and plot.

<<colon2>>=
# create the transition matrix
cstate <- c("stable", "progression", "death")
ctrans <- matrix(c(0,0,0,1,0,0,1,1,0), nrow=3,
                 dimnames= list(from= cstate, to= cstate))
ctrans
statefig(1:2, ctrans)  # visual check that it is correct

# Crude transitions, as a starting estimate
crude <-  c(.7, .1, 4)/(8*365)
cfit1 <- cmsh(Surv(time, status) ~ rx, id=id,  
              init=rbind(log(crude),0,0),
             qmatrix= ctrans, exact="death", data= cdata)
cfit1

surv1 <- predict(cfit1, type="pstate", newdata=dummy, p0=c(1,0,0),
                 time= c(1:45, seq(60, 8*365, by=60)))
plot(surv1, lty=1:2, col=c(1,1,3,3,2,2), lwd=2, xscale=365.25,
     xlab="Years from randomization", ylab="Probability in state")
legend("topright", c("Stable, placebo", "Stable, 5FU+Lev", 
                     "Progression, placebo", "Progresion, 5FU+Lev",
                     "Death, placebo", "Death, 5fu+Lev"), 
       lty=1:2, lwd=2, col=c(1,1,3,3,2,2),, bty='n')
@

The cmsh solution is not at all what we expected. The estimated treatment
coefficients are different than the Cox model and the predicted curves are
\emph{very} different: P(stable) drops almost immediately to zero. 
What happened?

The issue is that we have no intermediate values in the data: there are no
observations of ``stable'' after entry, and so the best MLE estimate for an
interval censored model is to assume that subjects switch 
immediately to the progression state.
The code iterates, increasing the stable:progression rate until the first 
progression, at 8 days, has a sufficiently large estimated Pr(progression)
to declare convergence of the log-likelihood. 
This first progression is on the 5FU+Lev arm, and that no surprisingly has 
the largest estimated 1:2 transition rate.  

To properly fit this data using an interval censored approach we should have
all the visits in the data set, then the actual time of progression for a
subject is more narrowly bracketed.
Per reference 1 in the help file for the data, patients were
assessed for progression every 8 weeks for the first year, every 4 months for
the second year, and every 6 months for years 3 to 5.  
An interesting exercise is to add psuedo observations that match
the visit schedule, one per progression, and observe how the estimate behaves. 
Since subjects cannot revert from progression, it suffices to add a
single pseudo visit prior each progression, e.g., id 1 had observed progression
at 968 days = 31.7 months, assume a non-progression visit 6 months before
at 786 days.
For progressions after 5 years perhaps assume yearly visits.

A second issue is that cfit1 implicitly assumes a constant baseline
hazard, i.e., the intercept (the cmsh function automatically adds an intercept 
term, like lm or glm does).
This would imply a linear cumulative hazard, which is inconsistent with
the Aalen-Johansen plot shown above. 
A piecewise linear with knots at 2 and 4 years might suffice? 
In an mshmm fit the underlying hazard, as a function of time, is an explicit
part of the model. This is unlike coxph where the baseline hazard function 
is implicit, and estimated non-parametrically after solving for $\hat\beta$.


\section{Dementia data set}
\begin{figure}
<<mcsa1, echo=FALSE>>=
mstate <- c("CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death")
mmat <- matrix(0, 4, 4, dimnames=list(mstate, mstate))
mmat[1,2] <- mmat[2,3] <- 1
mmat[1:3,4] <- 1
statefig(rbind(3,1), mmat)
@  
 \caption{State space model for the dementia data.}
 \label{mcsastate}
\end{figure}

We now return to the dementia data set, with an underlying biological
model shown in Figure \ref{mcsastate}.
First, look again, and more completely, at the observed transtions matrix.

<<dement1>>=
survcheck(Surv2(age, dx) ~1, id=ptnum, data = dementia)

survcheck(Surv2(age, dx, repeated=TRUE) ~1, id=ptnum, dementia)$transitions
@

As described in the help file for the data set, the on study, off study
and death data have been collapsed into a single variable \code{dx}.
The above table of transitions shows that it not uncommon for a patient's
diagnosis of MCI at one visit to revert back to CU at a later one.
If we fit a model corresponding to Figure \ref{mcsastate} using this
raw data, the result will be an estimated likeihood of 0, which would
be correct since the data contains an impossible (per the diagram)
transition.

A message which will be repeated multiple times is that you need to
create the transitions table and \emph{read} it.  Really read it, not
just a glance.  There are several things we can note from the above.
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \code{survcheck} routine was originally written for counting 
    process data.
    One aspect of Surv2, which translates from timeline form to that form,
    is the repeated argument.  When a subject has two sequential visits in
    the same state, the survfit and coxph routines need to know whether to
    treat the second observation as a new event. Most of the time they
    should not, repeated infections in the CGD data set is a counterexample,
    however.  In this data each row is a visit, the repeated form clearly shows
    that over half of all visit pairs are CU:CU; most patients stay stable for
    many years before progression.  The (any) row of the total visits table
    shows that 53\% (2296/4327) have no recorded changes of state during 
    follow-up.
  \item There are 30 instances of a CU:dementia pair.  Per the model we assume
    that these subjects passed through MCI, we simply did assess them often
    enough to observe the transient MCI state.
  \item Dementia:MCI is much less common than MCI:CU.  This reflects on one
    hand the clinical reality that CU and MCI are more difficult to distinguish
    than dementia, but also fewer overall dementia cases.
  \item There are no transitions from death to another state, or from
    death to censored (further follow-up after death).  This is as it
    should be.
  \item Off study dementia only transitions to death. This is because
    further record reviews are not scheduled for demented subjects.
  \item There are no transitions from nondem to nondem or off-dem. 
    This is, it turns out, an interesting artifact of how the online
    data collection system was designed.
    The nurse abstracters who review the history have a screen with fields
    for ``last assessment date for dementia' and 'dementia yes/no'.  
    At a second, third, etc review for a patient, should one occur,
    the contents of these are overwritten; thus the master data set
    at any given time only has the most recent assessment.
  \item More dementias were found in record review than in study visits.
    We believe that this reflects informative censoring: subjects whose
    cognition is slipping selectively drop out, i.e., exactly those most
    likely to receive a dementia diagnosis at their next visit. 
  \end{itemize}
Every real study has unique features and patterns like this.  Something that
looks odd, e.g., item 6 above, needs to be thought through: the ``feature''
you are seeing might be a simple programming error. 
The author's experience has normally been that the total number of calls to
survcheck (and other tabulations) while building a proper analysis data
set will exceed the number of calls to model fittting routines during analysis.

\subsection{Partial states}
For our first analysis we need to create two new variables
\begin{itemize}
  \item Sequential progression: the first diagnosis of MCI, dementia (or death)
  \item Off study diagnosis
\end{itemize}

The first is treated as a known state, the second as a marker. 

<<forward>>=
# first restrict the set of states, then stop backwards transitions
temp1 <- factor(ddata$dx, c("alive", "CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death"))
temp2 <- unlist(tapply(as.numeric(temp1), ddata$ptnum, function(x) {
               ifelse(is.na(x) | x==1, 1, cummax(x))} ))
ddata$maxdx <- factor(temp2, 1:5, c("alive", "CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death"))
with(ddata, table(dx, maxdx))
ddata$offdx <- factor(as.numeric(ddata$dx), 5:6, c("ND", "dementia"))

survcheck(Surv2(age, maxdx) ~ 1, ddata, id=ptnum)
@ 
The survcheck call verifies that no subject had more than 1 transtion to
MCI or dementia.
There were 29 transitions from MCI to dementia, so why did the number of MCI rows
increase by 38 in the prededing table?  
An example is subject 120 who has diagnoses of MCI, MCI,
dementia, MCI, MCI and death; they add 2 to the count.

The offdx variable will modeled as a marker, since it gives partial information
about the current state.
States that are observed are coded in the status portion of the Surv call on
the left hand side of the formula; incompletely observed states use the
marker argument, with a status of ``no precise state observed at this time'',
i.e., the first level of the maxdx variable (whatever label was chosen for
that level). 
The underlying model for a marker $y$ is that there is separate density of 
that marker $f_k(y)$ for each true state $k$. 
For the offdx marker the density is discrete and captured in a simple table.

The next step is to regularize the granularity of the age variable. 
As justification, imagine two subjects one of which appears every 15 months
on schedule and the other every 30 months, both of which start at age 60.
The age variable used in the model will be 60, 61.25, 62.5, 63.75, 65 for
subject 1, over their first periods of followup, while that for subject 2
will be 60, 60, 62.5, 62.5, 65 over the same interval.  
That is, subject 2 will be treated as though they are systematically just a bit 
younger. 
Now, over the period of active participation the variation in patient visits
is small enough that this minor difference won't matter, but it perhaps could
for two subjects with active vs off-study follow-up. Forcing the age covariate
to be on an identical schedule reflects a bit of extra care (and increases
the compute time).
The choice of single years is based on 200 years of shared experience using
population rate tables, which has clarified that single year data is a
sufficiently fine partition.

<<mscfexpand>>=
ddata2 <- survSplit(Surv2(age, maxdx) ~ ., ddata, id=ptnum,
                    cut=50:98)
ddata2$iage <- floor(ddata2$age)   #integer age
@ 

<<>>=
#make a new Q matrix
dstate2 <-c("CU", "MCI", "dementia", "death") 
q2 <- matrix(0, 4, 4, dimnames=list(dstate2, dstate2))
q2[1,2] <- q2[2,3] <- 1
q2[1:3, 4] <- 1
q2                 # and look at it

# Create a density for the discrete marker
offdens <- matrix(c(1,1,0,0, 0,0,1,0), 4, 2, 
                  dimnames=list(dstate2, c("ND", "dementia")))
offdens

# Do a constrained fit to the 4 state model
icoef3 <- coef(dfit1, matrix=TRUE)[,c(1,1,2,2,3)]
colnames(icoef3) <- NULL
rownames(icoef3)[2] <- "iage"
dfit2 <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage + male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4+ cmc,
    1:2+ 2:3 ~ male * (any.e4 + agrp) +  edu4+ cmc/ common), 
    data = ddata2, id = ptnum, qmatrix = q2, init = icoef3,
    marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))
dfit2b <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage + male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4+ cmc,
    1:2+ 2:3 ~ male * (any.e4 + agrp) +  edu4+ cmc/ common), 
    data = ddata2, id = ptnum, qmatrix = q2, init = dfit2,
    marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))

dfit3 <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage + male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4+ cmc,
    1:2+ 2:3 ~ male * (any.e4 + agrp)), data = ddata2, 
    id = ptnum, qmatrix = q2, init = dfit2,
    marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))

# initial coefficients based on a fit of the Minnesota rates
tdata <- data.frame(iage= 50:95,
                    rate= 365.25 *survexp.mn[51:96, 1, "2010"])
gfit <- glm(rate ~ iage, quasipoisson, data=tdata)
icoef4 <- cbind(coef(gfit), coef(gfit), coef(gfit), coef(gfit), coef(gfit))

dfit4a <-cmsh(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage, ddata2, id=ptnum, qmatrix=q2,
              init= icoef4, 
              marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))
              
dfit4 <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage + male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4+ cmc,
    1:2+ 2:3 ~ male * (any.e4 + agrp)), data = ddata2, 
    id = ptnum, qmatrix = q2, init = dfit4a,
    marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))
dfit4b <- cmsh(list(Surv(age, maxdx) ~ iage + male + any.e4 + agrp + edu4+ cmc,
    1:2+ 2:3 ~ male * (any.e4 + agrp)), data = ddata2, 
    id = ptnum, qmatrix = q2, init = dfit4,
    marker= state:offdx ~ 0/discrete(init=offdens))
@ 

The marker formula reflects the fact that in the likelihood the arrow of
probability goes from state to marker and not vice versa: P(observed marker) 
given the true state, not a distribution of states given the marker.
The \verb+~ 0+ states that there are no estimated parameters for this
discrete density, it will use the given initial values.

\begin{figure}
<<f2000>>=
counts <- with(subset(ddata, !duplicated(ptnum)),
               table(any.e4, agrp, male)) [,-4,]
cwt <- counts/sum(counts)

cdata <- expand.grid(any.e4=0:1, agrp=c("low", "moderate", "high"), male=0:1,
                     age=70, iage=70, edu4=3, cmc=0)
temp1 <- contrast(dfit0, cdata, weight=cwt, transition=1)
temp2 <- contrast(dfit1, cdata, weight=cwt, transition=1)
temp3a <- contrast(dfit3, cdata, weight=cwt, transition=1)
temp3b <- contrast(dfit3, cdata, weight=cwt, transition="2:3")

delta <- .25
yy <-c(1:12,14:25, 27:38, 40:51)
xx1  <- temp1[,1] + outer(temp1[,2], c(0, -1.96, 1.96), '*')
xx2  <- temp2[,1] + outer(temp2[,2], c(0, -1.96, 1.96), '*')
xx3a <- temp3a[,1] + outer(temp3a[,2], c(0, -1.96, 1.96), '*')
xx3b <- temp3b[,1] + outer(temp3b[,2], c(0, -1.96, 1.96), '*')
xx <- exp(rbind(xx1, xx2, xx3a, xx3b))
matplot(xx, yy, log='x', xlab="Hazard Ratio", ylab='', yaxt='n', type='n')
points(xx[,1], yy, pch=rep(c(19,1), each=6), col= c(1,4))
segments(xx[,2], yy, xx[,3], yy, col=c(1,4))
abline(h=c(13, 26, 39), lty=2)
z <- (yy[seq(1,48,2)] + yy[seq(2,48,2)])/2
axis(2, z, rep(c("low", "mod", "high"), 8), las=2, cex.axis=.6)
text(.25, c(12,25,38,51)-1, c("ND:dem, coxph", "ND:dem, cmsh", "CU:MCI, cmsh",
                            "MCI:dem, cmsh"), adj=0)
abline(v=1, lty=3)
abline(h=c(6.5, 19.5, 32.5, 45.5), col='gray', lty=3)
@ 
  \caption{Comparison of the coefficents for 3 models. The lower 2 panels
    show the estimate HR for the ND to dementia transtion, for a 
    transtion based (coxph) and state based (cmsh) model, respectively.
    The upper two show estimated HR for CU:MCI and MCI:death from a cmsh
    model. Black: any.e4=0, blue: any.e4=1, solid circle: female, open
  circle: male.}
  \label{hr2022}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{hr2022} has a lot of information. The lower panel mimics the top
panel of figure 2 in \cite{Jack22}, imperfectly of course since this data is
a simulant. We have not shown the transitions to death.
Panel 2 reveals that a fit treating the same set of states as interval censored 
has nearly identical coefficients. Since visit intervals in this data set are
fairly regular this is not surprising.

The top two panels show individual estimates for the CU:MCI and MCI:dementia
transtions. 
the same coefficients,
